<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad al-Badr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1926">1926</a>–<a href="/years/1996">1996</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad al-Badr was the last Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> and king of the Mutawakkilite Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. He succeeded his father Ahmad in September 1962 but was overthrown a week later by army officers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-al-sallal">Abdullah al-Sallal</a>, who proclaimed the Yemen Arab Republic. He led royalist forces backed by Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in the civil war that followed and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-badr.jpg" alt="Muhammad al-Badr" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_al-Badr.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad al-Badr was the oldest son of Ahmad bin Yahya, later Zaydi imam and king of North Yemen. After the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-muhammad-hamid-ed-din">Imam Yahya</a> in February 1948, al-Badr reached Sanaa but apparently gave only tacit support to the new regime. His father gathered the tribes around Hajjah, proclaimed himself imam and regained Sanaa within a month, executing the main perpetrators.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By late 1949 the prince, now called Sayf al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> al-Badr, was deputy over the port of Hodeida and Minister of the Interior. He played a prominent part in putting down the revolt of 1955 led by his uncle Sayf al-Islam Abdullah, and was then declared Crown Prince. He later became Minister of Foreign Affairs and, from 1958, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a>'s deputy over Sanaa. On 8 March 1958 he signed a pact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-states">United Arab States</a>, a federation linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in which Yemen kept its independence.</p><p>A great admirer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a>, al-Badr arranged for Egyptian experts to come and modernise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> in 1959, when he governed for a few months while his father was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for medical treatment; his father annulled this on return. After an assassination attempt in March 1961 left Imam Ahmad gravely crippled, al-Badr took effective control of the government in October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ahmad died in his sleep on 19 September 1962, and al-Badr was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> and king with the title al-Mansur. A week later, on 26 September, rebels shelled his residence, Dar al-Bashair, in Sanaa. Abdullah as-Sallal, whom he had appointed commander of the royal guard, staged a coup and declared himself president of the Yemen Arab Republic. The revolution announced that al-Badr had died under the rubble, but he had escaped unhurt to the north.</p><p>Tribes there pledged loyalty to him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> al-Mumineen, Leader of the believers, because the Zaydi Shia held loyalty to an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> descended from the Prophet's family to be a religious obligation. His uncle Sayf al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> al-Hassan, who had been proclaimed imam on the false news of his death, gave him allegiance, as did the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakil">Bakil</a> confederation and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hashid">Hashid</a>. The North Yemen Civil War began, with Saudi Arabia supporting al-Badr and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> the republicans.</p><p>In 1970, though most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>'s territory was still under al-Badr and the Hamid al-Din family, Saudi Arabia recognised the Yemen Arab Republic without consulting him, and the United Kingdom and other nations followed. He demanded to leave Saudi Arabia and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where he lived quietly in Kent. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1996 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad al-Badr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A week later army officers led by Abdullah al-Sallal overthrew his son <b>Muhammad al-Badr</b> and proclaimed the Yemen Arab Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/mutawakkilite-kingdom-of-yemen">Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen</a></li><li><blockquote>On 26 September 1962 army officers led by Abdullah al-Sallal overthrew Imam <b>Muhammad al-Badr</b> of the Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen and proclaimed the Yemen Arab Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/yemen-arab-republic">Yemen Arab Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sanaa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=15.35,44.2&amp;year=1971" aria-label="Sanaa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5757 1593 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Muhammad al-Badr"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6207 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6207 1863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=15.35,44.2&amp;year=1971">What was here in 1971? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
